{"id":"T1-heightfield","state":"awaiting_test","evidenceDir":"C:\\Users\\super\\OneDrive\\Documents\\Car Game\\coordination\\state\\T1-heightfield\\","summary":"I've written the two assigned files. **Nothing has been run:** I had no Luau runner and wasn't allowed to use Studio or the shell, so `selfTest` is NOT RUN and nothing is verified at runtime. Every other source file, model, init script and the test scene are untouched, and the module isn't wired into anything.\n\n## Files created\n\n**`src/shared/Config/TerrainConfig.luau`** holds the settings. All values are suggested defaults and still need your review in Studio.\n- **Units:** lengths, heights and widths are in studs; noise frequency is cycles per stud; slopes are rise over run (1.0 = 45°).\n- **Sections:**\n  - `Basin`: the valley floor and the ring of ridges around it.\n  - `Noise`: five noise layers (Warp, Ridges, Hills, Detail, Material).\n  - `Road`: Clearance 0.5, ShoulderWidth 10, TransitionWidth 30, MaxTransitionWidth 90, EmbankmentSlope 1.0.\n  - `Materials`: slope thresholds plus a mapping from class to `Enum.Material`. This is for painting terrain only.\n  - `Limits`: caps on input size and coordinates.\n\n**`src/shared/Gen/MountainHeightfield.luau`** is pure data: it creates no Instances and never writes to Terrain.\n```lua\nlocal field = MountainHeightfield.new(seed, circuit --[[or nil]], TerrainConfig --[[optional]])\nMountainHeightfield.heightAt(field, x, z)        -- final height\nMountainHeightfield.naturalHeightAt(field, x, z) -- height before road shaping\nMountainHeightfield.sample(field, x, z)          -- height, naturalHeight, slope, zone, blend,\n                                                 -- roadDistance, roadHeight, roadSegment, materialClass, material\nMountainHeightfield.selfTest()                   -- opt-in; returns (passed, lines); never prints\n```\n- **Input:** `circuit` is the table that `RoadGeometry.build` returns (`samples[i].position`, `width`). `closed` defaults to true; pass `closed = false` for an open road.\n- **Seed:** must be an integer, at most 2^53 in size. Each of the 6 noise layers gets its own seed, built from the seed, `GeneratorVersion` and a per-layer number. That seed sets a random offset and rotation for the layer. A list of tests is in the selfTest section below.\n- **Natural terrain:** a smooth bowl shape with a ridge ring at 320–620 studs from the centre. On top of that go sharp ridge noise (reduced on the valley floor), rolling hills and fine detail, all slightly distorted so the shapes look less regular. The centre is `Basin.CenterX/Z` if set, otherwise the average position of the road samples.\n- **Road blending:**\n  - It finds the closest point on the centreline segments, including the segment from the last sample back to the first, measured horizontally. The road height is interpolated along that segment.\n  - Out to half the road width plus the shoulder, terrain sits flat at road height minus `Clearance`.\n  - Beyond that it eases back to natural terrain over a blend width. The blend widens for big height differences, from `TransitionWidth` up to `MaxTransitionWidth`, so embankments stay near `EmbankmentSlope`.\n  - Past the blend, heights match natural terrain exactly.\n- **Material classes:** `RoadBed`, `Shoulder`, `Embankment`, `Rock`, `Meadow`, `Forest`. Rock is chosen by slope, with its threshold varied by its own noise layer.\n- **Deterministic and bounded:**\n  - Everything that affects output uses numeric loops, not table-order iteration.\n  - A spatial grid limits each query to the segments in one cell. When two segments are equally close, the lower index wins.\n  - Inputs are rejected with a clear message when they're bad: non-finite, out of range, missing or too short a road, or a road whose segments all have zero horizontal length.\n- **Cost:** `new` is O(samples × about 4–9 grid cells), capped by `MaxGridEntries`. `heightAt` is O(noise octaves + segments in one cell). `sample` is about 5× `heightAt`, because it estimates slope from neighbouring heights.\n\n## selfTest — NOT RUN\nIt covers repeatability, different seeds (next seed and negated seed), negative and far coordinates, clearance with interpolated road height, the wraparound segment (and its absence when `closed = false`), blend boundaries, continuity and bounds, 17 invalid-input cases, and finite values over a 49×49 grid and at the coordinate limits.\n\nTo run it from the Studio command bar:\n```lua\nlocal ok, lines = require(game.ReplicatedStorage.Shared.Gen.MountainHeightfield).selfTest() print(table.concat(lines, \"\\n\"))\n```\n\n## Risks\n1. **Untested:** the code has never been compiled or run, so syntax or type errors are possible. The continuity check's limit (1.5 studs per 0.25-stud step) depends on how steep the natural noise is. That's based on my estimate and may need adjusting.\n2. **Seams where two road sections meet:** if two stretches of road at different heights are both within the influence radius (115 studs by default), the height can jump where the nearest segment switches. PLAN.md's overlap check (70-stud spacing) doesn't rule this out. The circuit validator or a multi-road blend should handle it.\n3. **Bumps can poke through:** with 4-stud voxels, the 0.5-stud clearance is only kept exactly at the sample points. The terrain builder may need more clearance or a way to stop voxels rising above the road.\n4. **Possible platform differences:** `math.noise`, `cos` and `sin` could give slightly different floats on different platforms. That's fine if the server writes the terrain as PLAN.md says, but it's a risk if clients ever rebuild terrain from the seed.\n5. **Map edge:** the default basin centre is the road average (about (140, 30) for the test loop). The ridge ring reaches full height at 620 studs, so the hard edge of a 1200-stud map will still show.\n6. **Existing problem, not caused by me:** `src/server/init.server.luau` requires `SceneConfig`, `SceneLayout`, `TerrainBuilder` and `SceneLighting`, and none of those exist under `src/`. Codex should check this before the Studio tests. I didn't touch it.\n7. **Terrain material choices are suggestions only** and still need visual review."}
{"id":"T1-heightfield","state":"awaiting_test","evidenceDir":"C:\\Users\\super\\OneDrive\\Documents\\Car Game\\coordination\\state\\T1-heightfield\\","summary":"I've revised both assigned files to fix the jump Codex found. **Nothing has been run.** I had no Luau runner and wasn't allowed to use Studio or the shell, so `selfTest` is NOT RUN and nothing has been checked at runtime. No other source file, model, init script, test circuit, tool or report was touched.\n\n## Cause\nEach point took its height from the single nearest road segment. Wherever the nearest segment switched, the height stepped. That happens at the midline between two parts of the road (Codex's 4-stud jump), on the inside of a bend, and near the centre of a tight curve.\n\n## Changes\n\n**`src/shared/Gen/MountainHeightfield.luau`**\n- **Smooth blend between segments:** the distance to the nearest segment changes smoothly. Every segment that is only slightly farther away than the nearest one now gets a weight. That weight fades to 0 as the segment gets farther, so the road height under a point is a weighted average and never steps.\n  - Inside the asphalt and shoulder the fade-out distance is 0.5 studs, so clearance stays exact.\n  - Beyond the shoulder it grows 1 stud per stud. Ground between two parts of the road at different heights then changes over an embankment-like width.\n- **Shared sample points:** a segment whose closest point is its end only repeats the point it shares with the next segment. It fades out when that neighbouring segment covers the point. This stops graded roads skewing the height, and the nearest segment or point always keeps a total weight of at least 1.\n- **Grid search:** each query searches outward in rings of grid cells until no unchecked segment can matter, and never counts a segment twice. The result is the same whichever grid cell the point falls in.\n- **Conflicting corridors rejected:** `new()` errors if two different parts of the road have overlapping corridors and their heights differ by more than `Clearance`. No terrain can sit below both. Overlaps at the same height are accepted. Parts of the road less than about 80 studs apart along the road (half a turn around the corridor) count as the same stretch and aren't compared.\n- **Cost:**\n  - Each height query searches at most 7 rings of cells by default.\n  - `new()` is roughly samples × cells, plus the overlap check.\n  - Both are capped, and fail with a clear error when a limit is exceeded.\n  - The module header has the full complexity notes.\n\n**`src/shared/Config/TerrainConfig.luau`** adds four settings:\n- `Road.BlendBand = 0.5` (studs)\n- `Road.BlendBandGrowth = 1.0` (studs of fade-out distance per stud beyond the shoulder)\n- `Limits.GridCellSize = 32` (studs)\n- `Limits.MaxPairChecks = 4000000`\n\nAll are validated.\n\n## New `selfTest` checks (NOT RUN)\nAll earlier checks are still there. New ones:\n- **Codex's exact example** (seed 7, width 30, passes 80 apart, 8-stud height difference), on an open and a closed road:\n  - the 0.002-stud step across the midline;\n  - scans from one road to the other at three X positions;\n  - exact clearance in each corridor;\n  - a scan across the inside-corner bisector.\n- **Reversed sample order** gives the same heights.\n- **Grid cell edges:** steps of ±0.001 studs across cell boundaries in X and Z.\n- **A dense 5% graded road** with 4-stud samples:\n  - exact clearance between sample points;\n  - terrain never rises more than 0.025 studs above the clearance line (5% of the 0.5-stud `BlendBand`).\n- **Overlapping corridors:** rejected at an 8-stud height difference, accepted when level.\n- **Two new invalid configs** are rejected.\n- **Finite values** in the area between the two passes.\n- **The existing `Phase0Loop`** is built read-only through `RoadGeometry`, and the terrain is checked to stay below the road at the centreline and both edges.\n\nTo run it from the Studio command bar:\n```lua\nlocal ok, lines = require(game.ReplicatedStorage.Shared.Gen.MountainHeightfield).selfTest() print(table.concat(lines, \"\\n\"))\n```\n\n## Risks\n1. **Untested:** syntax or type errors are possible. The jump checks use a limit of 20 height studs per stud of distance, which I set from estimates.\n2. **`Phase0Loop` could be rejected** by the overlap check near its tight right-hander. By hand I estimate it passes, but I haven't verified it. If it fails, the selfTest line will show which segments and heights are involved.\n3. **Clearance isn't perfectly exact** within 0.5 studs of a sample point on graded road (error at most grade × 0.5, about 0.05 studs at 10%). The same applies on the inside of tight bends, where the road height under a point is genuinely ambiguous.\n4. **Steep ground between close roads:** two parts of the road close together at different heights get a steep but continuous slope between them. For Codex's example it is roughly 1:1.\n5. **Slower queries:** each lookup now considers more segments, and every query creates a small closure. Studio should measure the cost of a full-map build.\n6. **Last-digit differences:** sums are added in grid-search order, so heights may differ in the last floating-point digits between neighbouring cells. Results for a given point are still repeatable.\n7. **Earlier risks still apply:** possible `math.noise` differences across platforms, the hard edge of the map, and terrain material choices that still need visual review."}
